WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.557
The QLoRA switch.

2
00:00:01.837 --> 00:00:06.147
For a larger model, train against a 4-bit NF4 copy of the base.

3
00:00:06.427 --> 00:00:27.952
In TRL that is one more argument: build a BitsAndBytesConfig with load_in_4bit=True, bnb_4bit_quant_type="nf4", bnb_4bit_use_double_quant=True, and a 16-bit bnb_4bit_compute_dtype, and pass it to SFTTrainer as quantization_config along with your peft_config.

4
00:00:28.232 --> 00:00:33.928
TRL’s reference describes exactly that combination as QLoRA training.

5
00:00:34.208 --> 00:00:41.653
It requires bitsandbytes, which runs on NVIDIA CUDA GPUs and a few other backends, not on Apple Silicon.

6
00:00:41.933 --> 00:00:43.576
What changes in the results?

7
00:00:43.856 --> 00:00:56.677
Memory falls sharply for big models; step time may rise, since weights are dequantized on the fly; quality should be close to 16-bit LoRA, which the QLoRA paper reported for its setups.

8
00:00:56.957 --> 00:01:02.355
“Should be” is a hypothesis: if you switch, re-run the same evaluation and compare.

9
00:01:02.635 --> 00:01:15.819
The notebook’s last optional cell does exactly that: it builds the same trainer with a 4-bit NF4 base and scores it on the same test set, so you can put LoRA and QLoRA side by side for one model.

10
00:01:16.099 --> 00:01:17.059
For a 0.

11
00:01:17.339 --> 00:01:24.955
5B model the memory saving is small; the habit of checking whether a memory optimization changed quality is the point.
